WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Agent world extras.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.167
Nine frameworks and capabilities you will keep running into around the agent stack.

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:11.800
LlamaIndex is the data framework counterpart to LangChain.

4
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.611
It is strongest at ingestion, indexing,

5
00:00:14.611 --> 00:00:19.633
and query engines over your documents, though it has agents and workflows too.

6
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:23.300
You will see it named very often in RAG job descriptions.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.633
Haystack comes from deepset, a production-oriented pipeline framework for search and RAG.

8
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:31.500
You start with your documents.

9
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.433
They flow through explicit, typed pipelines.

10
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:37.763
And the result is search or RAG in production.

11
00:00:37.763 --> 00:00:40.267
It is popular in European enterprises.

12
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:45.833
Semantic Kernel is Microsoft's agent and orchestration SDK.

13
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:50.200
It targets the dot net and enterprise world, with Python support too.

14
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.100
If the employer is a Microsoft shop, this is their LangChain.

15
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.167
DSPy is the Stanford framework for programming, not prompting.

16
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:01.933
You declare typed modules, plus metrics.

17
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:06.467
Its optimizers then compile the prompts and few-shot examples for you.

18
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.900
It is the strongest answer to hand-tweaked prompt spaghetti.

19
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.800
These are structured-output tools.

20
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.273
Instructor patches LLM clients so they return validated Pydantic models,

21
00:01:18.273 --> 00:01:20.500
retrying when validation fails.

22
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.776
Outlines and Guidance go deeper and constrain decoding itself,

23
00:01:24.776 --> 00:01:28.367
so the output simply cannot break the schema or grammar.

24
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:34.467
OpenAI Swarm is a minimal, educational multi-agent library.

25
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:37.067
It is built from just agents and handoffs.

26
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:42.600
It is the precursor that grew into the Agents SDK, and it appears in older tutorials.

27
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.033
Computer use means a model operating a real graphical interface.

28
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:51.667
It looks at a screenshot, moves the cursor, clicks, and types.

29
00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:56.767
Anthropic shipped it first, and it is the frontier of an agent doing your actual work.

30
00:01:57.967 --> 00:02:00.099
Browser agents drive a web browser.

31
00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:03.633
They navigate, read the page structure, and fill in forms.

32
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:07.857
Examples include browser-use, Playwright-based stacks,

33
00:02:07.857 --> 00:02:11.200
and OpenAI's ChatGPT agent, formerly Operator.

34
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.700
Think of them as the practical subset of computer use.

35
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.067
A code interpreter lets the agent write and run code.

36
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:23.672
That sits on top of an isolated sandbox, such as Docker,

37
00:02:23.672 --> 00:02:27.900
gVisor, or Firecracker, or a service like E2B or Modal.

38
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.033
It is the most powerful tool you can hand an agent, and that is why sandboxing is non-negotiable.

39
00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:36.006
Let's recap.

40
00:02:36.006 --> 00:02:40.833
LlamaIndex, Haystack, Semantic Kernel, and DSPy are the frameworks to know.

41
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.800
Instructor, Outlines, and Guidance give you structured output.

42
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.033
Computer use and browser agents act on real interfaces.

43
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:51.967
And whenever an agent runs code, sandbox it.
